      "joined_by_ids": [],
      "local_path": null,
      "meta": {
        "timestamp": "2025-02-26T05:47:07.534071Z",
        "date_created": "2023-08-21T21:46:13.131788Z"
      },
      "ordering_key": 2,
      "per_curiam": false,
      "sha1": "",
      "snippet": "\nUtter, C.J.\n(concurring and dissenting) \u2014 As the majority explains, difficult line-drawing and careful adjustment of burdens of proof are necessary in order to resolve the inherent conflict between the public's constitutionally protected interest in open pretrial proceedings and the criminal defendant's constitutional right to a fair trial. Although agreeing with the majority's statement of ",
      "type": "in-part-opinion"
    },
    {
      "author_id": null,
      "cites": [],
      "download_url": null,
      "id": 9578538,
      "joined_by_ids": [],
      "local_path": null,
      "meta": {
        "timestamp": "2025-02-26T05:47:07.522383Z",
        "date_created": "2023-08-21T21:46:13.124951Z"
      },
      "ordering_key": 1,
      "per_curiam": false,
      "sha1": "",
      "snippet": "\nWilliams, J.\nThis case presents the question of whether a trial court properly barred the press and public from a pretrial suppression hearing. We hold that it did.\nThe stipulated facts giving rise to this action against a state officer are as follows:\nOn November 1, 1978, the State of Washington charged Jo Elliott Tharp with the crime of murder in the second degree, alleging that on March 30, ",
      "type": "lead-opinion"
    },
    {
      "author_id": null,
      "cites": [],
      "download_url": null,
      "id": 9578540,
      "joined_by_ids": [],
      "local_path": null,
      "meta": {
        "timestamp": "2025-02-26T05:47:07.545807Z",
        "date_created": "2023-08-21T21:46:13.136359Z"
      },
      "ordering_key": 3,
      "per_curiam": false,
      "sha1": "",
      "snippet": "\nDolliver, J.\n(dissenting) \u2014 The majority and the concurrence begin with an erroneous premise: that constitutional rights conflict. They thus reach an erroneous result: that a balance can and should be struck. As long as the court has the view that the constitutional right of the public (which includes the press, see Cohen v. Everett City Council, 85 Wn.2d 385, 535 P.2d 801 (1975)) to the open administration of ju",
      "type": "dissent"
    }
